    "result_text": "Green, and the audit record still shows the same `customerCode=NSW-16E6609E`, confirming behaviour is unchanged.\n\n## What I changed\n\nI agreed with all three findings \u2014 they're a single cohesion issue: once the per-city sequence (and its `clinicService.findAllOwners()` call) was gone, the customer-code assembly became a pure function of the owner's own data, so it no longer had a reason to live in the controller.\n\n- **Finding 1 \u2014 moved the assembly onto `Owner`.** Added `Owner#buildCustomerCode()`, which assembles `<REGION>-<HASH8>` from the owner's own region, telephone and last name. The controller now just calls `owner.setCustomerCode(owner.buildCustomerCode())`. The whole region-and-hash identity now sits beside the other derived identity accessors (`getLocality`, `getMembershipNumber`, `getCheckDigit`).\n- **Finding 2 \u2014 `deriveRegion()` is `private` again.** With the assembly on `Owner`, the controller no longer needs to reach it, so the model's public surface is back to the single semantic accessor `getLocality()`.\n- **Finding 3 \u2014 `sha256Hex8` travelled with the assembly.** It moved to `Owner` as a `private static` helper (and the crypto imports moved with it); the controller keeps its own `MessageDigest` usage only for the unrelated household-id hash.\n\nNo behavioural change \u2014 the output is byte-identical, and the acceptance suite is green.",
