    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe region-side extraction is good, but the customer-code identity logic is now split awkwardly across two units, and the split is what the change itself created.\n\n**1. `generateCustomerCode` no longer belongs in the controller \u2014 it has lost its only reason to live there.**\nPreviously this method had to be in the controller because it called `clinicService.findAllOwners()` to compute the per-city sequence. After this change, `generateCustomerCode(Owner)` uses *only* the owner's own data: `owner.deriveRegion()`, `owner.getTelephone()`, `owner.getLastName()`. It is now a pure function of the owner, and one half of it (region derivation) already lives on `Owner` alongside the other identity-derived accessors (`getMembershipNumber`, `getLocality`). A maintainer asking \"how is a `customerCode` formed?\" now has to look in two places, with both halves being pure owner-data logic. Co-locating the assembly on `Owner` (e.g. an `Owner#buildCustomerCode()` the controller calls in `setCustomerCode(...)`) keeps the whole region-and-hash identity cohesive and matches how the other derived identity fields already live on the model.\n\n**2. `deriveRegion()` was made `public` solely to serve the controller.**\nBecause the controller lives in `rest.controller.v1` and `Owner` in `model`, the extracted method had to be widened to `public` just so `generateCustomerCode` can reach it \u2014 adding a second public method that exposes the region concept next to the existing public `getLocality()`. If the code assembly moves onto `Owner` (finding 1), `deriveRegion()` can stay `private`, keeping the model's public surface to the single semantic accessor (`getLocality`) rather than exposing its internal computation step.\n\n**3. `sha256Hex8` is a generic crypto helper sitting in a REST controller.**\nIt is a pure, reusable digest utility with no HTTP/request concern. On its own this is minor and consistent with the old inline string-munging, but it is really part of the customer-code identity; if the assembly moves to `Owner` per finding 1, this helper should travel with it so all the identity-construction logic stays in one unit.\n\nNone of these are correctness issues \u2014 the pieces are each reasonable \u2014 but the net effect is that the identity-building logic got spread across the model and the controller precisely when the change removed the dependency that justified the controller half.",
